"193c650a-2471-5fc1-a082-f49a3c75fb59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119293 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119293 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119293 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53b26439-37ef-554d-b6f5-c8b08105b18e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119659 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119659 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n![](images/38/38e7866823d1b0a029cfc1d390bdcded9bbd8a79635ad80146efd600f19545dc.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119659 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b36a943c-f00a-5436-9256-0725a0008fdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119659 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1e/1ebbf14d4f46b9fa1f603e0ac116be9ba529afde630209db5fb5b982870dadec.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119660 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119660 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n![](images/41/41ea03b500685eb7ff881bb67aaa2fe3f612f5aad6d19d2a3d9c0365ba5278e2.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119660 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef028d89-e645-5ef3-a881-092292e2a271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119660 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/10/104f03abf423695444fe4fdbdc9043aed6dbbfd7dc3388115a19a4d679f1ba74.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119845 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119846 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1045 "The Dimension of a Span" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120056 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d014fcfb-88d3-58d4-928d-09436eaced72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120529 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120529 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120529 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120529 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "487f0ee8-db6c-59bd-83cb-1d43b5a535da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120529 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120732 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120732 #edb/ref 1061 19970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120732 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f0eb1a4d-9c4f-513f-be21-c3f099b4b267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120732 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120733 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120733 #edb/ref 1061 19971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120733 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c96304c7-f6d5-5dfd-b33f-9c76165825dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120733 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120734 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120734 #edb/ref 1061 19972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120734 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aa44487c-2db5-5c3c-bc3c-c62ca070f704" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120734 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120735 #edb/ref 1061 19974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120735 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c692fd19-bc87-5c2c-a172-5d1d8ce64f90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120735 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120762 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120762 #edb/ref 1061 25615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120762 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1510c9cb-5dac-5d6c-994f-d10e8a6d47b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120762 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121056 #edb/ref 1064 "There is something interesting about the bases of a vector space, summarized in the theorem below:\n\nIf the vector space $V$ has a basis with $n$ vectors, then any basis of $V$ has exactly $n$ vectors.\n\nThe number $n$ of vectors in any basis of $V$ is called the **dimension of the space** $V$ and is denoted $\\textrm{dim}(V).$\n\nFor example, consider the linear span below:\n![](images/3f/3f4139a1701c74e72ca55b662b1d5c06b02f1d3b6cab9a96b4667439dc196068.svg)\n\nWe know that, in order to find a basis of the span, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $3$rd columns. This tells us that\n$\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $H.$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, *any basis* of $H$ will also have *exactly* two vectors. So, we have $\\textrm{dim}(H)=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121056 #edb/ref 1065 3517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121056 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6700cc12-77d9-59b2-8ee8-826575be0c12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121056 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121057 #edb/ref 1064 "We can build some geometric intuition for finite-dimensional vector spaces. To illustrate, let's find the dimensions of $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\},$ $\\mathbb{R},$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nFirst, note that $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^1.$ We have the following standard bases:\n\n![](images/44/44f00659592bc6ac2742af0105fa493efa93e58e8dd63ee9af56a2d90b0e513a.svg)\n\nConsequently, we have the following dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}({R}^{0})=0,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{1})=1,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{3})=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's build some geometric intuition. We can represent the standard bases in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/16/169b5597d89fa32fd09772593cc2a9a330ee12881281dea4e3e43dc6165b9ebd.png)\n\nSo, the sequence $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ $\\mathbb{R}^1,$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ $\\ldots$ can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/37/379823eca26d43337744a8f4a3872e67ac537a6c193d55e39b5d6761f258f6f5.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^0$ is represented geometrically as a $0$-dimensional point.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^1$ is represented geometrically as a $1$-dimensional line segment.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is represented geometrically as a $2$-dimensional square.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is represented geometrically as a $3$-dimensional cube.\n\nUnfortunately, we can't visualize dimensions greater than $3,$ but we know that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ has the following standard basis consisting of $n$ vectors:\n\n![](images/c8/c8a606f297f86b56224cb9f50ba68b826e614d4f075b396a4104e19bc77c46e7.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n) = n.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121057 #edb/ref 1065 3518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121057 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "88c2cca4-9e7b-5ace-b239-798bcab0d26a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121057 #edb/ref 1067 "Geometric Intuition for Finite-Dimensional Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121069 #edb/ref 1064 "As defined earlier, the dimension of a vector space $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$.\n\nHowever, note that the definition above is only applicable if a vector space has at least one basis with a finite number of elements.\n\n- If a vector space has a basis with a finite number of vectors, then it is called **finite-dimensional**.\n\n- Otherwise, if there is no finite basis, the vector space is called **infinite-dimensional**.\n\nIn linear algebra, we mostly consider finite-dimensional vector spaces." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121069 #edb/ref 1065 4226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121069 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e433abf5-e941-5e63-9642-c3f90b4716a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121069 #edb/ref 1067 "Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317696 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317696 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317696 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2aab6359-d412-5594-9846-75e761e2adf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317700 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317700 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317700 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2bdefd21-573e-5f0e-84c7-9994c7e195f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317788 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317788 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317788 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4472fcff-17d4-5a02-a680-9de0b581a073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318384 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318384 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318384 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f75dceba-84c1-5c39-9174-0200a28709ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318414 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318414 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318414 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fde7d800-efa6-5d51-97dd-1d43ce1bba79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318590 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318590 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318590 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318590 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318590 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318590 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318590 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318590 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186317700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318590 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4af871db-2403-531b-aa56-f1be63167c90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319174 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319174 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319174 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319174 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319174 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbc23108-6d38-5169-be16-bd8ae0c5611e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319174 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319393 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319393 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319393 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0077e2b0-9780-54fe-afa4-b670dc9fed69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319510 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319510 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319510 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "267fce2f-8f79-53e9-831e-7c2a90e2adc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319525 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319525 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319525 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "29de170a-c6df-528c-9251-01f3de79ef6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319552 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319552 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319552 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "345eb41a-6222-5988-930d-6af162d31248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319566 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319566 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319566 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38e191bb-945f-5f74