592186347740 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347740 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347740 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186345623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347740 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4ee16fde-27c3-566c-9103-dab0b178ad0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347816 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347816 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347816 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186345688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347816 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "77c66077-e6a0-52cd-8409-bf48d80534b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348203 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348203 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348203 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348203 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348203 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3212ae7d-e55b-5563-bea8-a1958564eb0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348203 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $4$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $4$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410527 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410527 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410527 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8404cbe2-ec28-5347-a546-978b108f6afc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410531 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410531 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410531 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ae69705d-4393-52b2-abc9-696b673d6331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410536 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410536 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410536 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e419addd-03bc-5503-b8f2-4323b5a419e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410537 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410537 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410537 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e6ceea94-487e-5a46-b3ea-38f442055374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410538 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410538 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410538 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e773efeb-2aed-5d30-a63f-618047d3e38e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410542 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bc8cd6e0-fec4-59f6-bf63-f95c9e73406a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a087057-8a12-50fd-9bce-b13921276b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410545 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1045 "The Dimension of a Span" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186424598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424581 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "948a33eb-6d1f-58c5-9917-674dc68468c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424582 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a113c4d-a0ac-5651-906e-41d84f5d836e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424583 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424583 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424583 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5368a6fd-1c3c-5ec5-98c7-f3629d82df88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424584 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424584 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424584 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "73c49f7c-a1f3-56e9-95d3-4e8d7959ede8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424585 #edb/ref 1080 "0" 